{
  "title": "Modular exponentiation",
  "summary": "Compute a^b mod m in O(log b) by squaring the base and consuming the exponent bit by bit.",
  "complexity": "O(log b)",
  "tags": [
    "number-theory",
    "modular-arithmetic"
  ],
  "lessons": [
    "cp/math-basics/fast-exponentiation",
    "cp/math-basics/modular-arithmetic"
  ],
  "usage": "Reduce `a` modulo `m` first, keep every intermediate below `m`, and use `1 % m` as the initial result so `m = 1` returns 0. Products need 64-bit integers: with `m` up to about 3*10^9 the product of two residues fits in a signed 64-bit integer. For larger moduli use `__int128` in C++ or `Math.multiplyHigh` / BigInteger in Java. Python integers never overflow.",
